SM(527,{"n":"1 Corinthians 1","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 1.mp3","d":2839.3,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"We'll get started here in just a minute, and uh we won't be doing uh catch up really."],[26.64,"I will be doing introduction because we're starting St."],[29.68,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians."],[32.4,"So in just a minute we'll talk a little bit about Corinth and about this epistle in particular, and little bit about Second Corinthians, so not too much, because I'll want to blather on about that when we actually get, to 2nd Corinthians."],[46.96,"And I don't want you to be too bored when you have to hear it all a second time."],[51.04,"You're all nodding because you agree."],[53.04,"I know. So before I before I get into that though, is there are there any leftover questions from Romans or anything else before we dive in?"],[65.48,"Okay. So the city of Corinth is actually one of the most ancient and uh one of the most important in Greek history, but that city is technically not the city that St."],[78.84,"Paul visited and to which this epistle was written."],[82.6,"So the Corinth and that is at its location is not the on the location."],[87.16,"Well, yeah, well, no, it's in the same location, but I'll get into how that happened."],[92.04,"So uh Corinth was one of the earliest city-states uh in Greece."],[97.32,"If you look at a map of Greece, it sort of is at the juncture between sort of mainland Greece and the Peloponnese."],[105.76,"And so this made it a central location such that when there were threats to Greece as a whole, we're used to thinking of Greece as Greece."],[116.72,"You know, and we think about the ancient Greeks as the ancient Greeks."],[119.68,"But as you study ancient Greek history, you find out that there was no real Greece."],[125.52,"There were people who were ethnically related in various ways, but there were individual city states and their surrounding farmland that were independent entities."],[135.52,"And so, you know, in the sixth century, Athens and Sparta went to war with each other."],[140.56,"The Peloponnesian War."],[142.08,"So they then they did not generally get along."],[147.76,"That's the Spartans in particular were prone to raiding and enslaving their neighbors"],[155.56,"So, but and they all had, as you can imagine, if you've ever seen the movie 300, or if you've known anything about Sparta and Athens, they had very different cultures too."],[166.76,"We tend to think of ancient Greek culture like it was this monolithic thing."],[170.28,"We think about democracy and philosophy."],[172.6,"That was Athens."],[174.62,"And then if you think about the movie 300 and the Persian Wars, you might think about King Leonidas and the Spartans and their sort of warrior culture."],[183.58,"But they were very different."],[185.18,"And the same is true of the other Greek city-states."],[189.18,"But there were times, as when the Persians invaded a couple of times, and when they'd have other invaders from the north, when they would need to sort of put their differences aside for mutual defense."],[200.28,"To defend themselves against some outsider."],[203.24,"And the place where they would meet was Corinth, such that that League of Cities was referred to by the Greeks as the Corinthian League."],[213.0,"Because they would all gather at Corinth, being at that central location, they would gather there to discuss sort of things that affected everybody, to make treaties, to do that kind of thing."],[223.4,"So Corinth was sort of the symbol of whatever Greek unity there was to be had, was sort of centered in Corinth."],[231.8,"The other thing that Corinth was that ancient Greek Corinth was famous for, uh, was debauchery."],[240.24,"Corinthianized was used as a verb in ancient Greek to refer to being lewd or to be debauched, to be drinking and wine."],[253.52,"Sexually promiscuous, yes, chambering as the king."],[257.68,"Chambering and wine."],[258.8,"Yes. All those sorts of things were sort of synonymous with Corinth."],[264.8,"This was attributed by in in the classical period, so if we're talking about like in the fourth century, there was generally a view by the Greeks that sort of civilizations, when they were founded, sort of had a golden age."],[281.76,"And then it was sort of all downhill from there. in history"],[285.48,"So they they took it for granted that places like Corinth and Crete, because they were so ancient, because they were so old, that was why they were now very morally depraved."],[297.64,"Because their society had sort of slidden."],[300.84,"And there are still people who take that view of civilizations today."],[304.84,"It's sort of just all downhill from the founding."],[307.8,"But so that was the the usual explanation for why Corinthian society had become like that."],[314.52,"That was also that was not just we tend to think of culture as sort of a secular thing."],[320.77,"Because we're modern Americans, and so we have our American culture, and then we all have our different religious beliefs, and we think of those as separate things, that we all have our politics, which we think of as a separate thing."],[331.45,"Remember the ancient world, those were all mixed up together."],[336.33,"So your religion, which god or goddess or gods you worshipped was based on where you were from and who your family was and the way you interacted in your city."],[348.49,"Politics was local."],[350.09,"That's actually the origin of the word politics is polis, which means city."],[354.03,"Greek city state."],[355.47,"So all of that was connected and intertwined."],[359.71,"And so the ritual and religious festival life of Corinth was part of that sort of debauchery and promiscuity and and all of those things."],[372.99,"It was woven into the life of the whole community."],[378.47,"So you have these two things."],[380.07,"You have the city that's very important and that has this rich history and at the same time has sort of fallen off into wantonness."],[389.67,"New Orleans. Yeah."],[392.95,"Perhaps, yes, it was the New Orleans of."],[395.43,"Yeah. So all of that changed around 146 BC when the Romans were in the process of conquering the Greek cities and Greek city-states while they were on their way to conquering everything else."],[411.67,"And got to Corinth and decided we have an opportunity here to send a message, because of course, Corinth was the symbol of the unity of the Greek city-states against invaders, and so they leveled it to the ground."],[426.83,"Completely. Plowed it under."],[429.23,"This would later happen to Jerusalem at the end of the Barkokva Rebellion at the beginning of the second century."],[435.87,"The Romans did the same thing, leveled it to the ground."],[439.15,"They've already done it to Carthage at this point, haven't they?"],[441.79,"Yes. Yeah, they'd already done it to Carthage."],[444.59,"But unlike Carthage, with Jerusalem and Corinth and several other cities, they then built a new Roman city on top of it."],[451.63,"So you've displayed two things."],[453.71,"Number one, you know, here's the symbol of your strength, we just crushed it."],[458.91,"And number two, now we're going to put our own city where it used to be."],[464.29,"As a sort of demonstration of their power and domination to send a signal to the rest of the city states to just go ahead and surrender and capitulate so they wouldn't have the same thing happen to them."],[475.81,"And the Romans always had plenty of colonists to go and move into a place like that."],[481.17,"Because of course, when they level a city to the ground, that means anybody who's still there, when they get there, they're killing."],[487.17,"So the the Romans, of course, one of the one of the things you got when you got citizenship was a certain parcel of land."],[495.41,"Most people who gained citizenship who weren't born into a father who already had it."],[501.01,"Most people who gained citizenship in the Roman Empire gained it through military service."],[506.13,"So military veterans would serve a certain term."],[507.57,"At the end of their term, they'd be given a certain parcel of land"],[512.97,"And the way the Romans did this was they tended to do it at the edges, wherever the borders of the empire were."],[520.57,"They'd send these veterans out there in order to help spread the Roman culture and Roman rule, because they knew those military veterans would be loyal to the Roman state, loyal to Caesar, and help them keep control, keep control of the empire."],[536.49,"So that's what I mean when I say colonists, that's who they brought into refill, refill Corinth."],[544.49,"So the Corinth that St."],[546.81,"Paul visits, even though it's an ancient city, was only about 200 years old at the time he visited it, Roman Corinth."],[552.57,"And it's Roman Corinth to which he's writing"],[559.73,"Now that said that said, the residents of Corinth were very quick to sort of want to appropriate Corinth's history."],[570.21,"They were looking more at the positive aspects of it."],[572.93,"They also appropriated a lot of the negative aspects of it."],[578.13,"The central religious shrine in Roman Corinth was was the uh Temple of Demeter, who was a goddess of fertility and the harvest and grain, so obviously popular in an agrarian situation, but who had been associated with Corint Corh.inth as the goddess of for centuries"],[601.13,"The Temple of Demeter was both, at this time, in the Roman period, was both continuing sort of traditional Greek modes of sacrifice and worship on the one hand, and had also begun to take on aspects of the mystery cults in the Roman Empire."],[622.89,"Here's what that means."],[624.97,"Mystery cults were groups that incorporated traditional Greek and Roman religious observance with aspects of what we would call today like a secret society."],[638.11,"Or a club."],[639.25,"With a secret membership."],[641.97,"And much of that centered around often complex initiation rites."],[649.02,"To get in and to ascend through various levels of membership."],[652.94,"It was sort of like the pagan masons."],[655.66,"That may be redundant."],[657.34,"We won't talk about Freemasonry."],[659.58,"But um, but it was sort of that kind of idea that you'd have these initiations, you would become an initiate, and this was not a question of them revealing to you some secret knowledge, like, oh, we have these secrets."],[675.5,"Like you might think of Scientology today."],[677.66,"When you reach a certain level, they tell you, hey, there's aliens."],[681.2,"This isn't that."],[682.9,"It's not sort of information like that."],[684.9,"It these these. these rites, initiation rites were experiential"],[689.18,"They were aimed at usually at experiencing death and rebirth in some way."],[696.14,"They were often violent, frightening."],[698.86,"They'd involve sacrifices."],[701.1,"People would be, for example, a lot of the Mithras cults, they'd bathe people in the blood of a bull."],[707.5,"Yeah. So there were sort of these sort of extreme."],[712.62,"The idea was to produce these kind of extreme religious experiences or extreme spiritual experiences at ecstatic states through sort of ordeals, cold, lack of sleep."],[726.38,"These kind of things."],[727.9,"So parts of the cult of Demeter at Corinth had already started to assume some of these aspects."],[730.54,"There were these initiation rites"],[737.7,"So two elements of that that are going to be important for 1 Corinthians that we're going to come back to as we go through because it's going to be directly relevant to some of the things St."],[748.34,"Paul talks about in his letter to them."],[750.98,"The first is that estimates are in Corinth, in particular, about 90% of the meat in the meat market came from the temple of Demeter and the other temples."],[762.74,"Because they were offering daily sacrifices to the gods."],[767.78,"And so the Greeks used to joke, the more rhybald ones, or ribbled, about about. the uh the sacrifices, they referred to it as the burning of the bones"],[780.86,"Because they would essentially only burn the parts of the animal that they couldn't use for something else."],[786.48,"The priests would go and burn like the bones and some tissues they didn't want."],[792.1,"But they'd send all the leather to the tanner, and they all the meat would go to the meat market and all the right that wasn't eating."],[800.9,"So that was the only part that was actually off."],[803.22,"The parts that were offered to the god were the parts they didn't want, basically, and couldn't do anything else with."],[810.02,"This is the reason why, as an aside of the Old Testament, when you read through Leviticus, especially, there's all these rules about, you know, butchering the animal, and you take this. this part, and part, and that part"],[821.58,"That's why, because God is trying to communicate that no, you give God you give God, the true God, you give him the best part of your offerings."],[830.63,"You don't give him the leftovers once you've taken what you want."],[834.76,"But so they very much gave them the parts that they didn't want."],[839.4,"So, but the the the other thing to remember, we tend to think of sacrifices in terms of killing things."],[848.28,"Sacrifice is sacrificing something is not killing it."],[853.0,"Killing something is incidental to a sacrifice."],[857.96,"Because if you read, even in the Old Testament, you don't have to go and find weird books like I read about Greek sacries.ficial rit"],[865.28,"In the Old Testament, if you read the sacrifices closely, you'll see you don't always kill something."],[872.32,"There's lots of times when the sacrifices involve like wheat cakes or drink offerings, where you pour out wine."],[879.94,"Or grain offerings."],[881.78,"Those don't involve killing anything."],[883.94,"They're even sin offerings that don't involve killing anything."],[888.82,"So killing is incidental."],[890.42,"But what they always involve, what all of these sacrifices always involve, is food."],[895.64,"You don't sacrifice wood."],[898.0,"You don't say everything is food."],[900.54,"And part of it is offered to the God."],[905.02,"Part of it is eaten by the people who bring the offering, and part of it is eaten by the priests."],[907.58,"That's true in the Old Testament"],[910.98,"It's true for the Greeks, the Romans, the Phoenicians, the Assyrians, the Egyptians, everybody."],[918.98,"So sacrifices were meals that were shared by the community with each other, and by the community with the god they were worshiping."],[927.86,"That was seen to bind the community together with each other and with their deity."],[932.98,"Because they wanted the deity to be happy, be a happy part of the community because they were depending on, in this case, for example, Demeter, to give them a good harvest and to send rain."],[943.78,"So that's how that these sacrifices were conceived."],[946.74,"And so we've excavated the temple of Demeter at Corinth."],[952.42,"Huge dining halls"],[952.78,"And there are huge dining halls."],[956.78,"Most of the temple is places for people to eat at these feasts."],[962.15,"Where they would come and offer the sacrifices and then eat the meat."],[965.35,"And this is what linked the community of Corinth together."],[969.43,"But it also linked them together with this deity."],[973.91,"In the remains, by the way, we found all kinds of animals."],[978.71,"And so mostly it's it's pig bones, which is what we would expect archaeologically, because that was the number one animal for Greek sacrifices, was pigs."],[991.75,"That's because they breed relatively fast and grow fast, faster than sheep or calves or."],[997.11,"Or they weren't supposed to be"],[997.88,"So obviously Jews weren't eating that."],[1003.8,"But anyway, but no more is pigs, but there's there were all kinds of things there."],[1007.24,"There were the remains of sea urchins."],[1009.32,"There are remains of and we're not sure."],[1014.6,"We're not sure if they were sacrificing all those things, or if they were only sacrificing some of them, and then the other things were just food served at the banquets."],[1025.74,"What's called trapezamata, which are literally means things on the table."],[1031.98,"Along along with it."],[1034.06,"So what we'll get to a little later in 1 Corinthians, once we get into it, we'll get to a place where uh St."],[1040.86,"Paul. is directly comparing and contrasting that understanding of sacrifice with the Eucharist"],[1048.9,"Not saying that the Eucharist is different, but he basically understands the Eucharist in the same way."],[1053.38,"But we'll talk about that when we get there."],[1056.18,"Um so but that this is this is the central part of the life of the community."],[1063.3,"If Corinth is Greek New Orleans, then this is Mardi Gras."],[1067.8,"This is this is the right the the thing, are these feasts at the Temple of Demeter that bring everyone together."],[1077.32,"And this is obviously you could you're probably already thinking, you could probably already tell this is gonna cause a big problem for Christians who are living in the city."],[1085.64,"And so this is going to be one of the big issues that St"],[1086.9,"And it is."],[1090.34,"Paul's going to address repeatedly, not just in 1 Corinthians, but in 2 Corinthians also."],[1096.02,"This is a major, a major issue for them."],[1099.3,"So that's that's important to know in terms of uh that centrality uh in Corinthian life, the temple of Demeter."],[1108.18,"Um, as we saw St."],[1110.02,"Paul, as we saw in Acts, when we were reading through the Acts of the Apostles, St."],[1114.34,"Paul had visited Corinth."],[1116.02,"He had been responsible for sort of starting the Christian community there, starting with the synagogues and then and then preaching to the people."],[1125.3,"And so, unlike Romans, when we were reading through Romans, we commented and and St."],[1129.7,"Paul commented repeatedly that he hadn't been there yet, and he was writing to them in advance."],[1130.34,"Paul writing back to Corinth"],[1134.06,"This is St."],[1137.74,"So this was probably written in the early 50s AD."],[1141.98,"And Saint Paul is this is before his arrest, this is while he's still on his missionary journeys, but he's hearing reports, as we'll see, of things going on in Corinth that have him very concerned."],[1155.5,"And so he's writing back to them to address these things."],[1159.82,"As we talked about, we talked about Romans, all of St."],[1162.54,"Paul's epistles, they're not sort of theological treatises."],[1166.7,"They're they're letters to a church dealing with things that are actually going on"],[1172.15,"We're gonna see 1 Corinthians follows basically the same structure as Romans did, in that he's gonna start out talking about theology, talking about Christ, and then he's gonna take the theological things that he's outlining for them."],[1186.63,"Once he's taught them this, he's gonna say, Okay, so here's how this applies."],[1190.79,"There's gonna be the word therefore."],[1192.49,"There's gonna be a transition."],[1193.85,"And he's gonna say, Okay, here's how this applies to these things that are going on in your life and the life of your community."],[1200.09,"This is what this means."],[1201.93,"And we'll see that, we'll see that shift when we get there."],[1204.97,"It is likely that Saint Paul wrote at least four letters to Corinth."],[1210.73,"It's possible he wrote more."],[1213.29,"Uh, this is based on references that he makes in 1st and 2 Corinthians to to other letters that he's written to them that don't correspond the ones that we have."],[1225.91,"But he says in a previous letter in 2 Corinthians, and then says that he said da-da-da, and we look at 1 Corinthians and we're like, uh."],[1234.73,"So that implies there was one in between."],[1236.89,"So there were likely at least four."],[1238.65,"Some people hypothesize six or more, but that's just sort of all speculation."],[1243.87,"But it's the idea that there were probably four is probably accurate."],[1250.11,"That said, you may be thinking, well, we only have two of them."],[1253.79,"It's possible we have more than two of them."],[1255.95,"We'll talk about this more when we get to 2 Corinthians."],[1258.59,"But 2 Corinthians appears to be at least two of them put together."],[1263.11,"Meaning that at some point someone sort of collected sort of shoved them together, collected them together, two shorter letters together."],[1271.59,"Why would that have happened?"],[1272.63,"Well, there are a bunch of obvious reasons."],[1274.07,"We've talked about how much a copy of one of these books would cost."],[1279.92,"That a book the size of Romans would have cost the equivalent of about $5,000 at that time."],[1286.48,"So if you were paying for the materials and the time of the scribe, it'd be worth your while to not just get one short letter transcribed."],[1299.04,"If you could get two in one scroll, you'd want to get two, to get your money's worth."],[1304.94,"We find this a lot in early codices."],[1307.58,"The codex is basically our early primitive book"],[1310.8,"The earliest Christian books that we have, like of the scriptures from the fourth century, have all kinds of things in there."],[1319.04,"And it's not that they thought those were part of the part of the Bible."],[1323.1,"It's that they were paying a lot of money for that book."],[1335.02,"Throw in some of the church hymns in the back."],[1337.18,"So I, you know, so that I can, you know, have those."],[1341.52,"You're already shelling out a few more pages, isn't going to cost that much more."],[1346.28,"you You know know."],[1346.84,"Get everything you can in there."],[1348.72,"So that's a likely reason for them to have been brought together."],[1351.12,"It was probably also an early stage of all of St"],[1355.56,"Paul's letters being collected together."],[1358.84,"Uh by the year 100, St."],[1361.8,"Paul's letters were circulating together, all of them as one unit by the year 100."],[1369.0,"So that's not that long after the last one was probably written in the late 50s, early 60s, probably early 60s."],[1378.53,"And so within about 35, 36 years, the church had pulled them all together."],[1384.77,"They've gotten the letters from the different cities and put them together into a collection."],[1389.73,"Our earliest copy of that is called P46."],[1394.85,"P is short for papyrus, because that's what it's written on."],[1395.97,"It was found in Egypt"],[1400.65,"And it dates from somewhere in the first half of the second century."],[1405.13,"So somewhere between 100 and 150."],[1408.09,"Probably more toward 150."],[1411.05,"But still, that shows you how early we have a copy."],[1413.93,"And that's it, that's from Egypt."],[1415.85,"So not only had it been collected, but it had been collected and copies had gotten to Egypt."],[1422.57,"St. Paul didn't go."],[1423.77,"Didn't go by that time."],[1427.73,"Um, we'll see when we get to uh Second Peter that he actually refers to Saint Paul's letters as scripture."],[1434.57,"So this is not a late thing."],[1437.37,"This was recognized very early by the church that these were important, needed to be collected. together and uh and kept together"],[1444.93,"And so part of that may have been if we have you know four, five, six letters to the Corinthians starting to bring those together."],[1456.31,"Um, that may have been a step on the way, and so just our second Corinthians comes from a phase after two of them had been put together."],[1465.67,"That's not really a problem for us."],[1469.85,"Because we have the Bible that God wants us to have."],[1473.77,"So the fact that there was editing involved there."],[1476.97,"Sometimes our Protestant friends will talk a lot about the original."],[1480.43,"The original that St."],[1481.47,"Paul wrote. And I know I've talked about that before, but let's use 2 Corinthians for an example."],[1488.63,"What is the original?"],[1490.23,"Because St. Paul would have dictated it to a scribe."],[1493.35,"And so is what the scribe wrote down the first time, is that the original?"],[1497.59,"Because then the scribe would have given it to St."],[1499.51,"Paul and he would have read over it and edited it."],[1502.63,"So is it after he edits it?"],[1505.57,"The scribe would have taken it and read it to the church."],[1509.66,"So is it the way Corinth first heard it?"],[1512.94,"And if 2 Corinthians is two different letters put together, is the original those two letters separately that we don't have anymore?"],[1522.86,"Is it the first copy of them being put together?"],[1526.22,"That's the original."],[1527.56,"So the original."],[1530.16,"And if you start talking about the original of any of the old testament books, complete conjecture and guesswork."],[1536.64,"So the idea that there's some original, and that that's the authoritative one just doesn't work in reality."],[1545.22,"So, yes, there was a process of editing."],[1547.46,"St. Paul was part of that process of editing."],[1551.46,"And then the things being collected, and then ultimately, after being collected into St."],[1555.7,"Paul's Epistles is a collection being collected into the New Testament was editorial activity."],[1562.02,"So that's also the process by which 1 Corinthians was written."],[1565.64,"He had a secretary, he dictated it, he edited the copy."],[1569.16,"That secretary took it to Corinth, read it to them. and was expected to be able to answer questions"],[1576.16,"This particular one, with some of the things he has to say to Corinth, I don't know if I'd have wanted to be the secretary who had to go and read it to them out loud when they were gathered together because there's some tough stuff."],[1586.4,"I think I said last time as a teaser that uh 1 Corinthians is a good book to read whenever you think your church has problems, um, because no church around today has problems like Corinth had problems, as we'll see."],[1600.32,"And, you know, it's easy for us to be harsh and critical uh in that way, but we have to remember uh this is frontier Christianity."],[1611.5,"St. Paul at Corinth in particular is dealing with a lot of converts directly from paganism. as we're going to see as we go through 1 Corinthians"],[1620.42,"Meaning they're used to a whole different way of life."],[1625.14,"Okay. They literally, when they think of worship and ritual, think of going and having an orgy."],[1632.42,"Imagine going from that to being a Christian."],[1636.59,"There's gonna be a big adaptation."],[1640.93,"That's gonna have to happen."],[1642.45,"Their whole view, not just of their culture and their city, not just their religious views, their whole view of sexuality, their whole view of human life, and how it's to be conducted, and of morality, all of that has been completely changed to something completely foreign to them."],[1664.19,"They don't have any examples for what we're saying."],[1666.69,"And St. Paul wasn't able to stay there for 30 years to sort of guide them."],[1671.43,"He had to move on to go to other places."],[1673.43,"That's why he's sending these letters."],[1681.83,"Paul. First Clement is also written to the church at Corinth because there were still problems."],[1689.27,"So it's an ongoing thing."],[1692.63,"But this is people trying to understand a whole new way of looking at the world and life and everything, and adapt to that."],[1702.31,"And St. Paul is trying to help them. do that"],[1705.71,"Right? His goal is not to, he doesn't throw up his hands and say, okay, failed experiment."],[1712.67,"You know, I tried to start a church at Korith."],[1715.15,"It's a nightmare."],[1716.12,"I'm moving on."],[1717.88,"He's he cares for them, he loves them, he wants to help them do this."],[1723.96,"It's just they have a lot to do."],[1727.56,"And so most of the problems that he's going to have to address are places where they've taken their pagan view of the world, of religion, of the city, of life, of morality, and are just holding on to parts of it and don't understand that that's not compatible now with them living living as a Christian and that they that have they. have to leave, to leave that behind them"],[1756.8,"And to also be fair to them, part of the reason why it's easy for us to be critical is we look back and we look at Corinthian paganism."],[1767.46,"And it looks kind of bizarre and ugly and freakish to us."],[1772.36,"Especially the mixture of sexuality and religion, you know, which we would want nothing to do with."],[1778.18,"That looks so horrid to us that we say, Well, come on."],[1782.82,"You know, like, isn't it obvious that that this isn't?"],[1786.66,"And before we do that, uh, we should realize at least tacitly that in the same way. we are all functions of our culture"],[1797.26,"And the reason we're able to be so judgmental of them is that we don't really realize the way in which we as American Orthodox Christians are American."],[1809.66,"And have given have sort of carried our own cultural stuff with us into the church."],[1816.06,"And we don't have St."],[1817.42,"Paul writing a letter directly to us to kind of point it out, to kind of say, Hey, you know, I know you were raised to be a good consumer, but that's not how church works."],[1829.92,"Or I I know, I know you love freedom and democracy, but you don't get to vote on what the church believes and teaches."],[1837.52,"es. Y You know."],[1840.6,"Well, Paul had founded the church in Corinth, then."],[1843.09,"And he wrong."],[1845.09,"Somebody had to have communicated with him of the things going on in that area."],[1851.49,"Yes. Who was that?"],[1852.69,"Or we won't get to that."],[1854.13,"Well, we'll get to that sort of at the end, who some of those people might be."],[1857.97,"Because of course there are people who he knew while he was there."],[1860.37,"He did spend time there."],[1861.73,"So there are people he knew there who were still there."],[1864.82,"Well, that way of presentation, though, how would he how would they have kept up where he was at?"],[1870.82,"It would have been literal word of mouth."],[1872.58,"It would have been people traveling from place to place."],[1875.54,"It would have been people when you sent a letter, you sent a person carrying the physical letter."],[1880.9,"Yeah, there's no no post office, Pony Express."],[1884.22,"Yeah. So yeah, so it would have been I mean the main source probably would have been remember there's there's lively merchant trade around the Mediterranean, at this time, and Corinth is not an insignificant city."],[1898.24,"We know that, for example, uh St."],[1900.0,"Paul met Priscilla Naquila there, uh who he mentioned in Romans, who had gone from Rome to there and then back to Rome."],[1907.04,"Uh they were well to do, so they had business interests."],[1909.76,"So there there were people who were traveling and going back and forth for business reasons who St."],[1915.44,"Paul would have run into, you know, who he'd baptized, or you know."],[1921.04,"Even later in European history, if you know that somebody is is making a journey either to or past where you want to send somebody a letter, you get that person to take the letter."],[1932.7,"Even though that's not the person you're writing to."],[1935.5,"Or if you don't even if you don't have a letter, you would say, Oh, you're going to be able to do that."],[1940.54,"St. Paul's in Rome, go say like St."],[1942.46,"Paul always says at the end of his letter, say hi to this person, say hi to that person."],[1945.34,"And then he says, This person here with me greets you."],[1948.54,"You know. Um, but yeah, they just word of mouth say, say, well, go and visit, you know, when you're there, you know, go visit uh was it St."],[1957.42,"Paul and tell him, you know, send our greetings, send our, you know."],[1961.42,"And he may have asked, How are things going with the community in Corinth?"],[1967.18,"And then heard, unfortunately, what was going on."],[1970.54,"Uh, the community at Corinth"],[1973.3,"So, one more note, because it's gonna become important later on, not later on tonight, but much later on."],[1980.66,"St. Paul's epistles, and this is a reminder because we talked about this in the introduction to Romans."],[1984.74,"St. Paul's epistles are the earliest writings of the New Testament."],[1990.66,"We don't tend to think about them that way because they don't come first when we open up the New Testament."],[1995.64,"The Gospels come first."],[1996.84,"And what's in the Gospels happened first?"],[2000.73,"Christ's life obviously was before St."],[2004.65,"Paul did anything."],[2007.37,"But they weren't written down first."],[2011.39,"The first the first of the Gospels to be written was uh St."],[2014.11,"was. Mark's Gospel, and it probably written around 69"],[2018.95,"So we're talking about the early 50s for 1 Corinthians."],[2022.71,"So St. Paul's letters, Galatians is probably, which we'll get to probably in a few months."],[2029.67,"Galatians, which was the first one of St."],[2031.67,"Paul's epistles to be written, was probably written around 45 A.D."],[2035.75,"So that's about when the first book of the New Testament was written, it was about 45."],[2039.59,"But that's 24 years before St."],[2042.39,"Mark's Gospel. So obviously, the apostles are traveling and preaching, St."],[2046.95,"Paul's traveling and preaching."],[2048.95,"The story of the gospel of Christ's birth, life, death, resurrection, is being preached, proclaimed everywhere."],[2058.01,"So it's not that no one had heard that story."],[2058.81,"heard They'd all that story."],[2058.97,"of That's the basis the Christian community."],[2063.41,"And they were telling stories about Christ, quoting Christ, talking about his parables, preaching these things."],[2069.57,"But they existed in this sort of oral state until the gospel started being written."],[2074.93,"And what likely triggered the writing of the Gospels, in fact, we're told this by early church historians about Saint Mark in particular, who wrote the first one, what triggered it was likely the deaths of the apostles."],[2089.65,"When the apostles started to be, they were they were all martyred except Saint John, but as they started to be murdered, especially in the in the mid-60s under Nero, Saints Peter and Paul are uh martyred."],[2102.49,"There was a need to, hey, we've had the apostles who were first hand witnesses who were with Jesus to talk to and interact with and ask questions and teach us about these things."],[2114.25,"They're disappearing. We need to write this down."],[2118.59,"We talked about how it is written."],[2120.21,"Writing things down as a way of enshrining them."],[2123.3,"We need to now get these things written down and enshrined because we're not going to have the people here to directly interact with for much longer."],[2131.38,"So that's really what triggers that."],[2134.18,"But this is this is at the point where that is all going on orally."],[2138.34,"And the only things we have in terms of documents are these epistles of Saint Paul."],[2144.1,"So 1 Corinthians is not only going to tell us about Corinth in the middle of the first century, it's going to give us one of our best windows into the, for example, when he talks about the Eucharist, the liturgical life, the worship life of a Christian community at this early period."],[2165.28,"So we're talking about maybe 20 years after Christ's ascension."],[2169.92,"So what is what did that look like at that time?"],[2172.56,"This is our best and our earliest sort of witness to that, is uh Saint Paul's epistles."],[2178.96,"And as I mentioned in the introduction to Romans, uh a lot of people argue about whether St."],[2185.12,"Paul wrote all of his epistles."],[2187.76,"Uh 1 Corinthians, we'll talk more about that again when we get to some of the ones that people argue about."],[2192.48,"about. But for right now, 1 Corinthians nobody argues"],[2197.08,"Everyone accepts that this was Saint Paul."],[2200.92,"It's gonna have a little bit of a different flavor than Romans did, because remember, Romans was focused on this sort of one big issue of making sure we didn't end up with a Jewish church and a Gentile church."],[2213.5,"But making sure that we had one church."],[2216.7,"Sort of everything was aimed at that, as we saw in Romans."],[2220.78,"First Corinthians, as I said, we're gonna see a lot more of him interacting with former pagans."],[2226.4,"And trying to help them understand that."],[2228.4,"And it's aimed at sort of a whole laundry list of issues in the community."],[2234.08,"It's gonna be a little a little different because there's a different audience."],[2236.16,"'s a different audience. It that he's writing it to"],[2241.3,"So I'll let that be by way of introduction, and there'll be more stuff like that as we go through."],[2248.98,"So unless there are any questions or comments or yes, ma'am."],[2254.26,"Who would read these letters?"],[2257.06,"How would the people well it would it would be read to them?"],[2260.9,"Okay. So the the secretary, it was called the Greek word is emanuensis, if you want to be fancy."],[2269.22,"The scribe would take down the letter, go back and forth with St."],[2274.26,"Paul about editing it, and then he would be the one who would take it physically to Corinth."],[2279.06,"And he would go to the church during their gathering and instead of like a sermon that week, he would publicly read read the letter to everyone."],[2292.38,"Because of course, there was only a certain fraction of the community that would be literate."],[2297.9,"And as we said, copies were extremely expensive."],[2304.62,"Presumably, he would leave that copy there."],[2308.74,"But that's just one copy."],[2310.1,"It wasn't like he would show up and make copies for everybody in the church to take home and read."],[2314.82,"And yeah, but that that's an important point."],[2317.14,"That's an important point."],[2318.66,"Because we're so we live so far past the invention of the printing press that we we talk about reading the Bible and we take that for granted."],[2328.92,"But for most of the church's history, people didn't read the Bible."],[2334.44,"The way people received and interacted with the scriptures was in worship, having them read to them."],[2342.04,"And taught to them."],[2343.8,"So they were receiving it orally and conversationally."],[2347.48,"They weren't sitting there with their own copy, you know, reading it and thinking about it."],[2353.08,"You know, they didn't have Bible studies like this."],[2355.96,"So, but yeah, that just that just didn't happen."],[2359.56,"Um and even much later in uh church history, to give you an idea, the first printed Greek. New Testament in Greece"],[2373.02,"Printed book, not handwritten copy of the New Testament in Greece was printed in 1904."],[2384.94,"That's mostly because the Turks wouldn't allow them to print Bibles."],[2390.22,"But until 1904, if you went to a Greek church, the gospel book on their altar would be hand copied."],[2398.3,"And so that gives and that means the people who were poor, you know, under Turkish oppression, they didn't own a Bible."],[2407.02,"They didn't even own part of a Bible."],[2408.95,"And a lot of them were illiterate even if they had, but but they didn't own it and that's the beginning of the 20th century so yeah what we take for granted that's not how they would have interacted with this um they would have heard it and had it sort of read and preached and taught to them okay any other questions you sure okay then we'll go ahead and uh get started uh here in uh St."],[2446.67,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians 1 verse 1."],[2454.99,"Paul called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God and Sosthenes our brother."],[2464.07,"Okay. So we'll stop right there."],[2466.23,"So this is where you indicate who wrote it."],[2468.85,"So who's this Sosthenes fellow?"],[2471.17,"He's the scribe."],[2472.27,"He's the one who's remember it was Tertius who did uh Romans."],[2477.57,"In this case, it's this Sosthenes."],[2479.65,"Now it doesn't mean Sosthenes helped him write it, in the sense of like some of this is Saint Paul and some of this is Sosthenes."],[2489.03,"This is Sosthenes is the person who's coming and bringing it to you and reading it to you."],[2496.39,"Notice also uh how St."],[2499.11,"Paul describes uh him being an apostle."],[2501.59,"an He doesn't just say Paul, apostle of Jesus Christ."],[2510.72,"He says, called to be an apostle."],[2514.72,"And that word call, it's been a while since we talked about this."],[2518.08,"We talked about this way back in the long-ago time at the beginning of Acts, where we talked about St."],[2522.72,"Paul's quote-unquote conversion and how it wasn't really a conversion."],[2527.26,"That the way he describes it, if we look to the Old Testament, the way he describes what happened to him on the robe road to Damascus is right in the line with what happens in Isaiah 5 or 6, and what happens in Jeremiah chapter 1 when they're called. when God calls them to be prophets"],[2549.84,"They have experience where they see God enthroned, and God commissions them to be a prophet and to bring a message to a particular place."],[2559.76,"This is the experience that St."],[2561.44,"Paul had. On God's throne, who commissioned him to be an apostle to the Gentiles."],[2573.5,"Why does he say apostle rather than prophet?"],[2577.34,"Well, apostle uh means one sent."],[2581.98,"In the um, in his understanding, um they're very similar."],[2589.94,"So I don't want to give the impression that these are two different things."],[2594.95,"Um but there is a he's gonna use the phrase actually, I believe, in later in 1 Corinthians, prophets and apostles."],[2603.47,"Um prophets uh were called upon to sort of represent God to his people in a general sense, whereas apostle comes from the Greek verb apostelo, uh which means literally to send, like you're sent on a mission."],[2623.95,"So there's a sort of shade of difference there."],[2630.65,"Uh it's not a distinction, like they're two different things."],[2634.21,"But one is someone who's called upon to speak the words of God, to his people in a sort of general sense."],[2645.39,"There were prophets who were just prophets and occupied this particular place in the life of Israel."],[2652.65,"There was a person who was God's prophet."],[2656.57,"Whereas St. Paul, particularly here, is talking about this mission that he's been sent on by Christ."],[2665.95,"That it's not just a general thing."],[2667.55,"He was not called to stay in Jerusalem and serve this role in the life of the Christian community, but he was sent on this particular mission to the nations, to go out to the people of the nations and preach the gospel to them."],[2679.83,"Yeah, it's a shade of he's emphasizing one element of it."],[2684.71,"The Hebrew equivalent of apostle would be Shaliach, which interestingly later on came to mean lawyer."],[2697.11,"Later in Hebrew, which is where Shylock comes from."],[2699.99,"But that is where it comes from."],[2702.23,"It's a corruption of the yeah."],[2707.19,"But yeah, and so that that's a term that's sometimes used for the prophets, too."],[2714.08,"The person I sent to represent me."],[2716.8,"Um so yeah, it's a it's a shade of difference."],[2720.16,"But we also know that the uh eleven were referring to themselves as apostles um already by this time."],[2731.0,"And we'll see a little more of that later in 1 Corinthians and in Galatians, that that already was a term that referred to a particular office that they held."],[2742.44,"And so another thing that St."],[2744.28,"Paul is doing by using that particular description is saying, I'm a member of that group."],[2751.48,"We saw in Acts the prophet Agabas."],[2754.42,"Who brought a message from God to people, but was not considered one of the apostles in that sense."],[2765.26,"But so St"],[2768.5,"Paul already here is expressing how he sees himself as he's one who was called by Christ and then who was sent with this mission."],[2784.58,"Which means that that it's not about Saint Paul himself."],[2790.68,"What makes St."],[2791.4,"Paul important in St."],[2792.44,"Paul's mind, if anything makes him important, what makes him important is that Christ called him."],[2798.7,"And then what's important is the mission that Christ sent him on."],[2803.42,"So this isn't about St."],[2805.1,"Paul's authority. You better listen to me."],[2808.43,"But that look, Christ called me, Christ sent me."],[2812.07,"And this is the message he sent me to deliver to you."],[2816.87,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[2824.23,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2829.59,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"Intro to 1 Corinthians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No catch-up tonight; starting <b>St. Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Plan: a little on <b>Corinth</b>, this epistle, a bit on <b>2 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not too much - save it for 2 Cor so it isn't a \"second time\" bore</li><li class=\"d0\">Any leftover questions from <b>Romans</b>? - none</li></ul>"},{"t":65.48,"lv":2,"h":"Ancient Corinth vs. Roman Corinth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One of the <b>most ancient, important</b> cities in Greek history</li><li class=\"d1\">but technically <b>not the city Paul visited</b> - same location, different city (how below)</li><li class=\"d0\">Early <b>city-state</b>, at the juncture of <b>mainland Greece and the Peloponnese</b></li><li class=\"d1\">central location</li><li class=\"d0\">There was <b>no real \"Greece\"</b> - independent city-states + farmland</li><li class=\"d1\">ethnically related, not unified</li></ul>"},{"t":135.52,"lv":3,"h":"Corinthian League","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Athens vs. Sparta</b> - Peloponnesian War</li><li class=\"d1\">Spartans prone to <b>raiding and enslaving</b> neighbors</li><li class=\"d0\">Very different cultures - Athens = <b>democracy, philosophy</b>; Sparta = <b>warrior culture</b> (<i>300</i>, Leonidas)</li><li class=\"d0\">When <b>Persians</b> or northern invaders came → put differences aside for <b>mutual defense</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They met at <b>Corinth</b> → the <b>Corinthian League</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gathered to discuss common issues, <b>make treaties</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Corinth = <b>symbol of Greek unity</b></li></ul>"},{"t":231.8,"lv":2,"h":"Corinth famous for debauchery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Corinthianized</i> = Greek verb for being <b>lewd, debauched</b></li><li class=\"d1\">drinking, wine, sexual promiscuity</li><li class=\"d0\">Classical view: civilizations have a <b>golden age at founding, then downhill</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why places so ancient (<b>Corinth, Crete</b>) were seen as <b>morally depraved</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some still take this view today</li><li class=\"d0\">Audience aside: \"the <b>New Orleans</b> of\" Greece</li></ul>"},{"t":314.52,"lv":3,"h":"Religion, politics, culture all one","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We treat <b>culture, religion, politics</b> as separate - because <b>modern Americans</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient world: all <b>mixed together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">your god/goddess based on <b>where you were from, who your family was</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>politics</b> from <i>polis</i> = <b>city</b>, Greek city-state</li><li class=\"d0\">So Corinth's <b>ritual and festival life</b> was part of the debauchery</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>woven into the life of the whole community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two things: <b>important, rich-history city</b> + fallen into <b>wantonness</b></li></ul>"},{"t":395.43,"lv":2,"h":"146 BC: Romans level Corinth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>146 BC</b> - Romans conquering the Greek city-states</li><li class=\"d0\">Corinth = symbol of unity against invaders → \"send a message\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>leveled to the ground</b>, \"plowed it under\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Later same at <b>Jerusalem</b> after <b>Bar Kokhba Rebellion</b> (early 2nd c.); earlier <b>Carthage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unlike Carthage, Jerusalem and Corinth got a <b>new Roman city on top</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two messages: <b>\"we just crushed your symbol\"</b> + <b>\"our city now sits where it was\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">signal to other city-states to <b>capitulate</b></li></ul>"},{"t":475.81,"lv":3,"h":"Veteran colonists","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Romans always had <b>colonists</b> to move in</li><li class=\"d1\">anyone left when they arrived was killed</li><li class=\"d0\">Citizenship usually gained through <b>military service</b></li><li class=\"d1\">end of term → <b>parcel of land</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Veterans settled at the <b>edges of the empire</b></li><li class=\"d1\">spread <b>Roman culture and rule</b>; loyal to <b>Caesar</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They refilled Corinth</li><li class=\"d0\">→ the Corinth Paul visited was only <b>~200 years old</b>: <b>Roman Corinth</b></li></ul>"},{"t":559.73,"lv":2,"h":"Temple of Demeter and mystery cults","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Residents quick to <b>appropriate Corinth's history</b> - positive and negative</li><li class=\"d0\">Central shrine: <b>Temple of Demeter</b> - goddess of <b>fertility, harvest, grain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tied to Corinth for <b>centuries</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Roman period: <b>traditional Greek sacrifice</b> + aspects of <b>mystery cults</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mystery cults</b> = traditional religion + <b>secret society / club</b> - \"the <b>pagan masons</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">complex <b>initiation rites</b>, ascending levels</li><li class=\"d1\">not secret <i>knowledge</i> (not Scientology's \"aliens\") - rites were <b>experiential</b></li><li class=\"d1\">aimed at experiencing <b>death and rebirth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">violent, frightening; <b>sacrifices</b>; Mithras cults bathed in <b>bull's blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ecstatic states</b> by ordeals - cold, lack of sleep</li><li class=\"d0\">Demeter's cult at Corinth already taking on these aspects</li></ul>"},{"t":750.98,"lv":2,"h":"Meat from the temples","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Two elements important for <b>1 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First: ~<b>90% of the meat</b> in Corinth's meat market came from <b>Demeter's and other temples</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>daily sacrifices</b> to the gods</li><li class=\"d0\">Greeks joked: \"<b>burning of the bones</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">burned only parts they couldn't use; <b>leather to the tanner</b>, <b>meat to market</b></li><li class=\"d1\">god got the parts they <b>didn't want</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>Leviticus</b> has so many butchering rules</li><li class=\"d1\">God = give the <b>best part</b>, not leftovers</li></ul>"},{"t":839.4,"lv":3,"h":"Sacrifice is a meal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sacrificing ≠ killing</b> - killing is <b>incidental</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT: <b>wheat cakes, drink offerings, grain offerings</b>, even some <b>sin offerings</b> with no killing</li><li class=\"d0\">Always involves <b>food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">part to <b>God</b>, part to the <b>people</b>, part to the <b>priests</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same for Greeks, Romans, Phoenicians, Assyrians, Egyptians</li><li class=\"d0\">Sacrifices = <b>meals shared</b> by community with each other and with the god</li><li class=\"d1\">binds community <b>together and with deity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Demeter to give <b>harvest and rain</b></li></ul>"},{"t":946.74,"lv":3,"h":"Excavated dining halls","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Temple of Demeter at Corinth</b> excavated: <b>huge dining halls</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most of the temple = places to <b>eat at feasts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Link of the community of Corinth - and with the deity</li><li class=\"d0\">Remains: mostly <b>pig bones</b>, number one Greek sacrificial animal</li><li class=\"d1\">breed and grow <b>fast</b>; Jews wouldn't eat them</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>sea urchins</b> etc. - unsure if sacrificed or just banquet food</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>trapezamata</i> = \"things on the table\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Later in 1 Cor: Paul <b>compares and contrasts</b> this with the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not saying Eucharist is different - he understands it <b>the same way</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1056.18,"lv":2,"h":"Feasts at Demeter = Mardi Gras","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Central part of community life: \"If Corinth is Greek <b>New Orleans</b>, this is <b>Mardi Gras</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Obvious <b>big problem for Christians</b> living in the city</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul addresses it <b>repeatedly</b> - 1 <b>and 2 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Major issue for them - note the centrality of the <b>Temple of Demeter</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1108.18,"lv":2,"h":"Paul and the Corinthian church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul visited Corinth</b> (Acts) - started the church there</li><li class=\"d1\">began with the <b>synagogues</b>, then preached to the people</li><li class=\"d0\">Unlike <b>Romans</b> (hadn't been there yet), now <b>writing back</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Written <b>early 50s AD</b>, before his arrest, still on <b>missionary journeys</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hearing <b>reports</b> of things in Corinth - very concerned</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>theological treatises</b> - letters to a church about <b>real problems</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same structure as Romans: <b>theology/Christ first</b>, then <b>\"therefore\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"here's how this applies to your life and community\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1204.97,"lv":2,"h":"Four letters; 2 Corinthians as composite","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul wrote <b>at least four letters</b> to Corinth, maybe more</li><li class=\"d1\">references to <b>other letters</b> in 1 and 2 Cor that don't match ours</li><li class=\"d1\">says \"in a previous letter...\" and we say \"uh\" → <b>one in between</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some hypothesize <b>six or more</b> - speculation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Corinthians</b> appears to be <b>at least two letters put together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a copy of a book like Romans ~ <b>$5,000</b> equivalent</li><li class=\"d1\">worth putting <b>two in one scroll</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Early <b>codices</b> (4th c.) contain all kinds of things - hymns in the back</li><li class=\"d1\">not thought of as Bible - \"<b>get everything you can in there</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1351.12,"lv":3,"h":"Collection of Paul's letters; P46","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Early stage of <b>collecting all Paul's letters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">circulating <b>together as one unit by AD 100</b></li><li class=\"d1\">only ~<b>35 years</b> after the last (early 60s)</li><li class=\"d0\">Earliest copy: <b>P46</b> - <b>papyrus</b>, found in <b>Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>100-150</b>, probably nearer 150</li><li class=\"d1\">collected and copies already reached <b>Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Peter</b> calls Paul's letters <b>scripture</b> → not a late thing</li></ul>"},{"t":1465.67,"lv":2,"h":"The \"original\" and editing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not a problem - \"we have the <b>Bible God wants us to have</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestants talk of <b>the original</b> - what is it for 2 Cor?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>scribe's first draft</b>? → Paul <b>edited</b> it?</li><li class=\"d1\">as the scribe <b>read it to the church</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">two letters <b>separately</b>, now lost? first copy <b>put together</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">OT books: \"<b>complete conjecture and guesswork</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ no single authoritative original; real <b>process of editing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul part of it → collection → <b>New Testament</b></li><li class=\"d0\">1 Cor same: <b>secretary dictated, Paul edited</b>, secretary took it to Corinth, read it, <b>answered questions</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tough stuff - wouldn't want to be the secretary reading it aloud</li></ul>"},{"t":1586.4,"lv":2,"h":"Frontier Christianity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Good book to read <b>when you think your church has problems</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"no church around today has problems like Corinth\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Remember: <b>frontier Christianity</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>converts directly from paganism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worship = \"going and having an <b>orgy</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">whole view of <b>sexuality, human life, morality</b> changed</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>examples</b>; Paul couldn't stay <b>30 years</b> to guide them</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Clement</b> also written to Corinth - <b>ongoing problems</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul doesn't call it a \"<b>failed experiment</b>\" - he <b>loves</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems = holding on to parts of <b>pagan view</b> of religion, city, life, morality</li><li class=\"d1\">not compatible now</li></ul>"},{"t":1756.8,"lv":3,"h":"We are products of culture too","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Corinthian paganism looks <b>bizarre, ugly, freakish</b> to us</li><li class=\"d1\">before judging: we are <b>functions of our culture</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>American Orthodox Christians</b> carry our own cultural stuff in</li><li class=\"d0\">No Paul writing to us: \"you were raised a good <b>consumer</b>, but that's not how church works\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you love <b>freedom and democracy</b>, but you don't <b>vote on what the church believes</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1840.6,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: how did Paul hear?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Who told him what was going on?</li><li class=\"d1\">people he knew there who stayed; more at the end</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: How did they keep up?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>word of mouth</b>, people traveling; letter carried by a <b>person</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no post office, <b>Pony Express</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lively <b>Mediterranean merchant trade</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Priscilla and Aquila</b> - met at Corinth, Rome and back; well-to-do, <b>business interests</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's greetings at end of letters - \"this person here greets you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Someone asked \"how's the community in Corinth?\" → heard what was going on</li></ul>"},{"t":1973.3,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's epistles are the earliest NT writings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reminder from Romans intro: <b>earliest NT writings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gospels first in order and in events, <b>not written first</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mark</b> first Gospel, ~<b>69</b>; <b>1 Cor</b> early 50s</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Galatians</b> (in a few months) first epistle, ~<b>45 AD</b> → first NT book</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>24 years</b> before Mark</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel <b>preached everywhere</b> - <b>oral</b> until written</li><li class=\"d0\">Trigger for writing: <b>deaths of the apostles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all martyred except <b>John</b>; <b>Peter and Paul</b> under <b>Nero</b>, mid-60s</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we need to write this down\" - <b>enshrine</b> it</li></ul>"},{"t":2138.34,"lv":2,"h":"What 1 Corinthians gives us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Only documents then = <b>Paul's epistles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Best window into <b>Eucharist, liturgical life, worship</b> ~<b>20 years</b> after the ascension</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Authorship</b>: some argue about other epistles, but for 1 Cor <b>nobody argues</b> - all accept Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Different flavor from <b>Romans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Romans: one issue - <b>one church</b>, not Jewish and Gentile</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor: <b>former pagans</b>, a <b>laundry list</b> of issues; different audience</li></ul>"},{"t":2248.98,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: who read the letters?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Who would read these letters?</li><li class=\"d1\">read <b>to</b> them - <b>amanuensis</b> (Greek: scribe)</li><li class=\"d1\">scribe took dictation, <b>edited with Paul</b>, carried it to Corinth</li><li class=\"d1\">at the gathering, <b>instead of a sermon</b>, read it publicly</li><li class=\"d1\">only a fraction <b>literate</b>; copies <b>expensive</b>; one copy left there</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of church history: people <b>didn't read</b> Bible - <b>heard it in worship</b>, taught</li><li class=\"d1\">no printing press, no Bible studies like this</li><li class=\"d0\">First <b>printed</b> Greek NT in <b>Greece</b>: <b>1904</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Turks wouldn't allow Bibles printed; altar Gospel book <b>hand copied</b></li><li class=\"d1\">poor owned <b>none</b>; many illiterate</li></ul>"},{"t":2446.67,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 1:1 - Paul and Sosthenes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:1</b>: \"Paul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, and <b>Sosthenes</b> our brother\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Indicates <b>who wrote it</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sosthenes</b> = the <b>scribe</b> (Romans: <b>Tertius</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">not co-author - he <b>brings and reads</b> it to you</li></ul>"},{"t":2510.72,"lv":3,"h":"Called to be an apostle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Called\"</b> - recall <b>Acts</b>: Paul's \"conversion\" wasn't really conversion</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Isaiah 6</b> and <b>Jeremiah 1</b> - see God <b>enthroned</b>, commissioned</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>road to Damascus</b>: commissioned as <b>apostle to the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why <i>apostle</i> not prophet? <b>sent</b> (<i>apostello</i>)</li><li class=\"d1\">very similar - not two different things; \"<b>prophets and apostles</b>\" later in 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">prophets = <b>represent God to his people</b> generally; apostle = <b>mission</b> he's been sent on</li><li class=\"d1\">not to stay in <b>Jerusalem</b> - sent <b>to the nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew equivalent <b>shaliach</b> → later \"<b>lawyer</b>\" (Shylock)</li><li class=\"d1\">used of prophets too: \"the person I <b>sent to represent me</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>The eleven</b> already called themselves apostles - an <b>office</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul says: I'm a member of that group</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Agabus</b>: prophet, not an apostle in that sense</li></ul>"},{"t":2768.5,"lv":3,"h":"Not about Paul's authority","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul expresses how he sees himself: <b>called by Christ, sent with a mission</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What makes Paul important: <b>Christ called him</b>, and the <b>mission</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"you better listen to me\" - <b>\"Christ called me, Christ sent me\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this is the <b>message he sent me to deliver to you</b></li></ul>"}]});
